      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. You've confirmed it, but the reason you gave is about timing, and it doesn't explain why this dose is needed. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen has no documented benefit over 3,200 mg/day. Analgesic and anti-inflammatory effects plateau at lower doses, while the risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events keeps rising. Entering it could cause real harm to the patient.\n\nI can enter either of these right now:\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day, the maximum), with a note to monitor renal function and GI symptoms.\n- **Ibuprofen 600 mg PO four times daily** (2,400 mg/day), if you want a lower dose that's still effective.\n\nIf pain control is the aim, I can add a second agent, such as scheduled acetaminophen, to the order. If there's a specific indication that calls for supratherapeutic dosing, the best route is to discuss it with pharmacy. They can review it and help get it through the hospital's high-dose override process.\n\nTell me which option you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
